{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "82e3d6cf-095f-46eb-8348-d1b0addd6529",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Notebook 8 – Correlation & Covariance "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "850ea1a9-e7b1-4e8e-94bb-a101baab9fa8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Correlation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "76b7ede6-846a-4e8f-91e4-5a829fc8fffb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Correlation is a statistical measure that tells us the strength and direction of the relationship between two variables\n",
    "\n",
    "The correlation value is between -1 and +1.\n",
    "\n",
    "+1 → Perfect positive correlation\n",
    "0 → No linear correlation\n",
    "-1 → Perfect negative correlation\n",
    "\n",
    "Simple Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose:\n",
    "\n",
    "HoursStudied\tExam Marks\n",
    "  1\t              40\n",
    "  2               50\n",
    "  3\t              60\n",
    "  4\t              70\n",
    "  5\t              80\n",
    "As study hours increase, marks also increase.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, they have a positive correlation.\n",
    "\n",
    "Business Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "A company may check the relationship between:\n",
    "\n",
    "Advertising expenditure ↔ Sales\n",
    "\n",
    "If advertising increases and sales also increase, there may be a positive correlation.\n",
    "\n",
    "AI/ML Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Correlation is useful during feature selection.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example:\n",
    "\n",
    "House Size ↔ House Price\n",
    "\n",
    "If they have a strong correlation, house size may be a useful feature for predicting house price"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "45e38cd3-5e37-480e-837e-6e51b23b4eb3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Correlation: 1.0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np                              #PythonImplementation\n",
    "\n",
    "hours = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n",
    "marks = [40, 50, 60, 70, 80]\n",
    "\n",
    "correlation = np.corrcoef(hours, marks)[0, 1]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Correlation:\", correlation)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e70396a1-29ae-4c64-89ef-b86fd1b6e857",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Covariance"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dcbdce61-8ef8-4639-a8aa-ccb69dcd2ee8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Covariance measures how two variables change together.\n",
    "\n",
    "It tells us whether two variables tend to move:\n",
    "\n",
    "In the same direction → Positive covariance\n",
    "In opposite directions → Negative covariance\n",
    "Formula\n",
    "\n",
    "For sample data:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Cov(X,Y)=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(X_i-\\bar{X})(Y_i-\\bar{Y})}{n-1}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Numerical Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose:\n",
    "\n",
    "X = [1, 2, 3]\n",
    "Y = [2, 4, 6]\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Mean:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$ \\bar X = 2 \n",
    "$$Yˉ=4\n",
    "\n",
    "Business Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Covariance can be used to study:\n",
    "\n",
    "Advertising Spend ↔ Sales\n",
    "\n",
    "AI/ML Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Covariance is important in techniques such as PCA (Principal Component Analysis), where relationships between variables are analyzed"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "bf391b95-83cf-4ec2-8865-b8f2a33b5207",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Covariance: 2.0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np                 #PythonImplementatiom\n",
    "\n",
    "X = [1, 2, 3]\n",
    "Y = [2, 4, 6]\n",
    "\n",
    "covariance = np.cov(X, Y)[0, 1]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Covariance:\", covariance)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "bdcb3678-a4df-4f00-a193-5cb77810a214",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Positive Correlation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "7580d4fe-fcff-46ef-b551-e4cc17e7075d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "Positive correlation means that when one variable increases, the other variable generally also increases.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Study Hours ↑ → Marks ↑\n",
    "\n",
    "Business Example:\n",
    "    \n",
    "Advertising ↑ → Sales ↑\n",
    "\n",
    "AI/ML Example:\n",
    "    \n",
    "House Size ↑ → House Price ↑"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "a360b531-b621-4c9a-b836-273cde61491e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Positive Correlation: 1.0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np                              #PythonImplementation\n",
    "\n",
    "hours = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n",
    "marks = [40, 50, 60, 70, 80]\n",
    "\n",
    "correlation = np.corrcoef(hours, marks)[0, 1]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Positive Correlation:\", correlation)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e2ba0be3-ce21-422f-b465-cad9c903e7fe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Negative Correlation\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "83f44455-01b9-4a58-b8ef-6e83729c1210",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "Negative correlation means that when one variable increases, the other variable generally decreases\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Price ↑ → Demand ↓\n",
    "\n",
    "Business Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Product Price ↑ → Number of Customers ↓\n",
    "\n",
    "AI/ML Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Two features may have a strong negative relationship:\n",
    "\n",
    "Age of Car ↑ → Car Value ↓"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "757c5fe7-7a42-4680-9f28-8c93209d77f5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Negative Correlation: -1.0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np                     #PythonImplementation\n",
    "\n",
    "price = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\n",
    "demand = [90, 80, 70, 60, 50]\n",
    "\n",
    "correlation = np.corrcoef(price, demand)[0, 1]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Negative Correlation:\", correlation)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fd9608fc-6890-493a-944a-df6ac3d16544",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### No Correlation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "0bad8379-b6f0-49e4-a81e-3de9a54a3710",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "No correlation means there is little or no linear relationship between two variables\n",
    "\n",
    "For example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Shoe Size ↔ Exam Marks\n",
    "\n",
    "There may be no meaningful linear relationship\n",
    "\n",
    "Correlation = 0 does not always mean there is absolutely no relationship.\n",
    "\n",
    "There could be a non-linear relationship"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "67b812f9-b4c4-4506-bba5-11a8ecb77000",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Correlation: -0.13987572123604705\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np                   #PythonImplementation\n",
    "\n",
    "x = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n",
    "y = [7, 2, 9, 1, 6]\n",
    "\n",
    "correlation = np.corrcoef(x, y)[0, 1]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Correlation:\", correlation)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ea2224df-c93f-4b91-afd6-8a848ea96619",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Pearson Correlation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4f6c21b2-2630-4536-a0dc-f64295616245",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Pearson correlation measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two numerical variables\n",
    "\n",
    "It is commonly represented by:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$ r $$\n",
    "\n",
    "Formula:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "r=\\frac{\\sum (X_i-\\bar X)(Y_i-\\bar Y)}\n",
    "{\\sqrt{\\sum(X_i-\\bar X)^2\\sum(Y_i-\\bar Y)^2}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "When to use Pearson?\n",
    "\n",
    "Use Pearson when:\n",
    "\n",
    "Variables are numerical\n",
    "\n",
    "You are interested in a linear relationship\n",
    "\n",
    "Data is reasonably suitable for Pearson's assumptions\n",
    "\n",
    "Business Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Advertising Spend ↔ Sales\n",
    "\n",
    "AI/ML Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Checking correlation between numerical features before building a model"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "id": "b1043b7e-df8c-4cba-9e5f-e11b7a0ee4ea",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Pearson Correlation: 1.0\n",
      "P-value: 0.0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from scipy.stats import pearsonr                  #Pythonimplementation\n",
    "\n",
    "x = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n",
    "y = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\n",
    "\n",
    "r, p_value = pearsonr(x, y)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Pearson Correlation:\", r)\n",
    "print(\"P-value:\", p_value)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4c228ffb-f57e-4db8-bd84-1d23c9871ca6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Spearman Correlation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "49eab8d8-7076-442d-a8a2-e796c8a2bf89",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Spearman correlation measures the relationship between the ranks of two variables\n",
    "\n",
    "It is useful when the relationship is monotonic but not necessarily linear, or when the data is ordinal/ranked\n",
    "\n",
    "It is represented by:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$ \\rho $$\n",
    "\n",
    "Formula:\n",
    "\n",
    "For data without tied ranks:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\rho=1-\\frac{6\\sum d_i^2}{n(n^2-1)}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "\n",
    "\\(d_i\\) = Difference between the two ranks\n",
    "\n",
    "\\(n\\) = Number of observations\n",
    "\n",
    "Business Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Customer rankings:\n",
    "\n",
    "Customer Satisfaction Rank ↔ Customer Loyalty Rank\n",
    "\n",
    "AI/ML Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Spearman correlation can help analyze ordinal features or relationships that are monotonic but not linear."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "id": "190a9b1d-347c-4767-a077-46b0d05f312b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Spearman Correlation: 0.9999999999999999\n",
      "P-value: 1.4042654220543602e-24\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from scipy.stats import spearmanr              #PythonImplementation\n",
    "\n",
    "x = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n",
    "y = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\n",
    "\n",
    "correlation, p_value = spearmanr(x, y)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Spearman Correlation:\", correlation)\n",
    "print(\"P-value:\", p_value)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b71de6fa-81a3-4761-9436-ea68311d5494",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Correlation Matrix"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "fd1b69e5-556e-4408-81d6-9abff5d7191f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "A correlation matrix shows the correlation between multiple variables at the same time\n",
    "\n",
    "For example:\n",
    "\n",
    "        Age   Salary   Experience\n",
    "Age      1     0.7        0.8\n",
    "Salary  0.7     1         0.9\n",
    "Exp     0.8    0.9         1\n",
    "\n",
    "AI/ML Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "A correlation matrix can help identify highly correlated features.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Age ↔ Experience\n",
    "\n",
    "If two features are extremely correlated, they may provide overlapping information"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "id": "ac62eee5-4708-4c70-a746-51f4cf122bee",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "                 Age    Salary  Experience\n",
      "Age         1.000000  0.999714    0.993009\n",
      "Salary      0.999714  1.000000    0.993293\n",
      "Experience  0.993009  0.993293    1.000000\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "\n",
    "data = {\n",
    "    \"Age\": [22, 25, 30, 35, 40],\n",
    "    \"Salary\": [25000, 30000, 40000, 50000, 60000],\n",
    "    \"Experience\": [1, 3, 5, 8, 12]\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "df = pd.DataFrame(data)\n",
    "\n",
    "correlation_matrix = df.corr()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(correlation_matrix)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "26662776-bd0e-4c5e-8d1e-7344b8cadc68",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Heatmap"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "59b34910-50a6-40ff-8410-57170ceb5599",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "A heatmap is a graphical representation of data where values are represented using different shades/intensities/\n",
    "A heatmap is a visual representation of data using different colors.\n",
    "\n",
    "A correlation heatmap makes it easy to see relationships between many variables\n",
    "\n",
    "Business Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "A company can use a heatmap to examine relationships between:\n",
    "\n",
    "Advertising\n",
    "Sales\n",
    "Price\n",
    "Customers\n",
    "Revenue\n",
    "\n",
    "AI/ML Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Before training a machine-learning model, a correlation heatmap can help identify:\n",
    "\n",
    "Strongly related features\n",
    "    \n",
    "Potential redundant features\n",
    "    \n",
    "Positive relationships\n",
    "    \n",
    "Negative relationships"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 14,
   "id": "4b1ba7ba-293e-4521-abdd-9509218e43be",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd                        #PythonImplementation\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "\n",
    "data = {\n",
    "    \"Age\": [22, 25, 30, 35, 40],\n",
    "    \"Salary\": [25000, 30000, 40000, 50000, 60000],\n",
    "    \"Experience\": [1, 3, 5, 8, 12],\n",
    "    \"Performance\": [60, 65, 75, 85, 90]\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "df = pd.DataFrame(data)\n",
    "\n",
    "correlation_matrix = df.corr()\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.heatmap(correlation_matrix, annot=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.title(\"Correlation Heatmap\")\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "a0a0e628-ed87-4646-8de7-7d618a0d78ae",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "What does annot=True mean?\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.heatmap(correlation_matrix, annot=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "annot=True displays the actual correlation values inside the heatmap."
   ]
  }
 ],
